-------------------------- Minimax Optimization ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Given your current implementation (alpha-beta pruning + iterative deepening + basic heuristic), here's what's worth learning next, roughly ordered by impact:

Speed
Move ordering — the biggest single speedup available to you. Alpha-beta pruning is most effective when the best moves are searched first. If you always search the best move first, alpha-beta approaches O(b^(d/2)) instead of O(b^d) — effectively doubling your searchable depth for free. Look up:

Killer move heuristic — store the 2 moves that caused a beta cutoff at each depth and try them first next time
History heuristic — track how often each move caused a cutoff across the whole search and use that as a sorting key
Your old moveComparator was the right idea but had a side-effect problem (it called applyMove inside a sort comparator). The killer/history approaches are cheaper and don't have that issue.

Transposition table with Zobrist hashing — the same board position can be reached by different move orderings. Without a cache, you re-evaluate it every time. Zobrist hashing gives you a fast, cheap hash of any board state, and you store (hash → score, depth, flag) in a hash map. Look up: transposition table, Zobrist hashing.

Aspiration windows — in iterative deepening, instead of searching depth N+1 with the full [-∞, +∞] window, use a narrow window around the score from depth N. Most of the time it succeeds and you get a faster search; if it fails you re-search with the full window. Look up: aspiration search.

Accuracy
Quiescence search — probably the most important accuracy improvement. Right now when you hit depth 0 you call evaluate, but the position might be "unstable" (e.g., the opponent is one move from winning). Quiescence search extends the search past depth 0 for "forcing" moves only (immediate wins/threats) until the position is quiet. This eliminates the horizon effect where the AI doesn't see a threat just beyond its search depth. Look up: quiescence search, horizon effect.

Negamax — your current minimax has separate maximizing and minimizing branches. Negamax rewrites this as a single recursive case using score = -negamax(...), which is cleaner, less error-prone, and how most chess/game AI literature is written. Once you understand negamax, reading about advanced techniques becomes much easier. Look up: negamax.

Fork/double-threat detection in the heuristic — a position where you have two simultaneous threats (the opponent can only block one) is essentially a forced win. Your heuristic doesn't currently weight this. A simple version: count lines where you have 3-in-a-row; if there are two or more immediate threats in one position, add a large bonus. Look up: threat-space search, double threat.

Good resources
Chess Programming Wiki (chessprogramming.org) — written for chess but almost everything applies directly to your game. Start with the pages on alpha-beta, move ordering, transposition tables, and quiescence search
"Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach" (Russell & Norvig) — Chapter 5 covers game search thoroughly with good pseudocode
Minimax series by Sebastian Lague on YouTube — short, visual, beginner-friendly walkthrough of minimax → alpha-beta → improvements
Priority if you can only do one thing
Quiescence search for accuracy (stops the AI from missing threats just past the search horizon), move ordering with killer moves for speed (cheap to implement, dramatic improvement to alpha-beta efficiency). Do those two before anything else.
